  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 200 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nFriday Night through Tuesday:\n\nSynopsis: Upper low pressure will migrate from the Northeast into \nthe Canadian Maritimes through the weekend, with a building ridge \nbecoming the predominant upper level synoptic-scale feature through \nthe beginning of next week. Forming beneath the building upper \nridge, broad high pressure will become established from the western \nGreat Lakes into the Ohio River Valley Sunday into Tuesday. \nPositioning of the surface high will allow chilly northerly surface \nwinds in place at the beginning of the period to gradually be \nreplaced by milder southerly flow during the Monday-Tuesday time \nframe. Thus expecting high temperatures to gradually trend back \ntoward and above normal through the beginning of next week. With \nupper ridging and surface high pressure dominating regionally, not \nanticipating any appreciable rainfall chances through the duration \nof the long term period. \n\nFriday Night: Expecting the coldest window of the period as winds \ntaper & mostly clear skies prevail. Probabilistic guidance shows \nhigh (~80%+) potential for lows at or below 30 in most locations \naway from the immediate Lake Michigan shoreline, and medium-high \n(~50-70%) potential for lows at or below 25 to the west of I-41. \nWhile winds will be tapering, lingering breezes could support wind \nchills in the upper 10s and low 20s. Bundle up if planning to be \noutdoors. \n\nQuigley",
